<rst>
    <header>
        <relations>
            <rel name="antithesis" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="background" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="concession" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="condition" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="elaboration" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="evidence" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="nonvolitional-cause" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="summary" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="volitional-result" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="conjunction" type="multinuc"/>
            <rel name="contrast" type="multinuc"/>
        </relations>
    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="47" relname="span">Upon first glance the Medicare site is extremely unappealing.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="48" relname="conjunction">Many things about the site are confusing,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="48" relname="conjunction">and there is an abundance of information on the main page along with split areas to review.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="5" relname="antithesis">Despite the overwhelming amount of information provided on the primary page,</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="49" relname="span">information at the bottom of the site forces the user to scroll down.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="7" relname="nonvolitional-cause">Often the elderly have problems with dexterity and the motor skills necessary to perform this action due to arthritis, other physical limitations, or inexperience with a keyboard or mouse (Hanson, 2004).</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="50" relname="span">The benefits of less maneuvering cannot be discounted when developing a website for use by the elderly.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="44" relname="antithesis">The Medicare web developers seemed less concerned with aesthetics than presenting all the information possible.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="44" relname="span">However, the elderly user is more easily confused</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="9" relname="condition">if the information seems overwhelming (Karvonen, 2000).</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="43" relname="contrast">Web design for the elderly demands simplicity and information that is distinctly separated is easier to navigate and comprehend.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="43" relname="contrast">The Medicare site seems very disorganized with not much thought given to commonly needed information.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="39" relname="span">Another possible issue for elderly users is the lack of standardization on some of the linked pages.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="15" relname="nonvolitional-cause">The left hand menu was not accessible on some of the pages, possibly to cut down on distractions from the information</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="40" relname="span">but the user could not easily return to the main page or find the information they were viewing before.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="17" relname="nonvolitional-cause">Often elderly users have memory impairments,</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="41" relname="span">so offering multiple ways to return to previous information will enhance the user experience.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="36" relname="span">The appearance and functionality of the Medicare website might have been improved by the use of a portal based system allowing the user to select several different options via GUI that would provide a more customized experience.</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="18" relname="elaboration">For instance, certain text colors or font types might be easier to read for certain users who are visually impaired.</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="36" relname="elaboration">Additionally, certain background colors might appeal to the user more or result in less eye strain overall.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="35" relname="elaboration">The key would be to design the GUI with ease of use for a wide range of user experience levels.</segment>
        <segment id="22" parent="23" relname="concession">Web designers tried to meet the needs of the intended users,</segment>
        <segment id="23" parent="37" relname="span">but due to the diversity of elderly users the site must meet the skills, expectations, and needs of those intended users (Myers, 1994).</segment>
        <segment id="24" parent="32" relname="span">One positive attribute is the mouse-over function for multiple selections on the left hand portion of the site.</segment>
        <segment id="25" parent="24" relname="evidence">The selections are easy to make because they stay available without having to move over them quickly.</segment>
        <segment id="26" parent="32" relname="volitional-result">The designers definitely were concerned with ensuring elderly users could make the selections without the frustration of having to struggle with the mouse and the menu items if they disappeared.</segment>
        <segment id="27" parent="55" relname="span">The Medicare site would be much more useful to elderly citizens</segment>
        <segment id="28" parent="27" relname="condition">if the web designers would attempt to be more creative in developing a site that would build confidence in users and provide information on Medicare benefits in a clear and organized manner.</segment>
